  "follows the transcription language, because that is the language your commands arrive in": "",
  "Use the engineer as the coach": "",
  "on by default: one person, doing both jobs. Turn it off to give the coach its own name and voice — worth it if you want to know which of them is talking without listening to the words": "",
  "what the coach is called, and what it answers to": "",
  "Say the analysis out loud": "",
  "off leaves the diagrams and nothing else — the panel is the other half of coaching and it is silent. A segment then sits in the middle of the queue for as long as \"On screen for\" says and moves on, because there is no longer anything to wait for": "",
  "Call mistakes as they happen": "",
  "a locked wheel or a trip off the road, named in three words while it is happening — separate from the analysis above, which is a paragraph at the corner exit about something already over. Turn this off if being told you locked a wheel while you are still gathering it up is not what you want": "",
  "fewer words everywhere, on top of whatever the driver level already trims from a critique": "",
  "Fallback voice": "",
  "used only for driver names, which no pack can contain": "",
  "a recorded voice pack — the only thing that sounds like a person": "",
  "Pace": "",
  "-10 to 10 for the fallback voice; blank is the default of 3, which is brisk. A voice pack is recorded and cannot be sped up": "",
  "(let Windows choose)": "",
  "(no pack)": "",
  "Coaching": "",
  "Watch a rival": "",
  "follows the driver ahead in your class on its own — or the one behind when you are leading it — and says where they were quicker each lap": "",
  "Corners": "",
  "every place the road bends, whether or not you slow down for it": "",
  "Straights and flat-out sections": "",
  "off by default: there is usually nothing to teach on a straight. Worth turning on where the exit before it matters more than the corner itself — a tenth lost down the Hangar Straight was lost at Chapel, and the straight is where it shows": "",
  "Driver level": "",
  "how much is said about each segment. Every level finds the same faults — they say less about them. \"Beginner\" gives what went wrong, what it cost and what to do; \"intermediate\" drops what it cost; \"advanced\" drops the fix too. Worth moving up where the corners come quickly enough to keep the queue full": "",
  "Segment analysis": "",
  "what went wrong through each corner, once you are clear of it. The main thing the coach is for. The section above chooses which segments it looks at; this is whether it speaks about them": "",
  "Mistakes as they happen": "",
  "a locked wheel, a trip off the road, losing the back end — called the moment it happens rather than at the end of the segment, because by the next corner it is history. Turn off if you would rather hear about it once, in the analysis": "",
  "Where they were quicker, each lap": "",
  "the summary against the driver you are studying, once per lap of theirs. The longest thing the coach says, so worth turning off on a circuit where the corners come quickly": "",
  "On with the session": "",
  "off by default: it is a practice tool, and a note every braking zone for two hours at Le Mans is not what anybody wants. Turning it on here takes effect straight away, and it stays on across restarts": "",
  "Note volume": "",
  "0 to 1, and separate from the engineer's own volume because the two are mixed rather than queued — a note sounds over whatever is being said. A note only has to be noticed, where speech has to be understood": "",
  "Size": "",
  "pixels square. Small puts a thirteen-metre track on screen as a hairline and nothing about car placement can be read from it": "",
  "On screen for": "",
  "seconds. Long enough to look at through a corner exit, short enough that it is gone before the next one": "",
  "Opacity": "",
  "0 to 1. Near solid, because one alpha covers the whole window — a see-through panel is a see-through road": "",
  "Pinned": "",
  "unpin to drag the panel where you want it, then pin it again. While it is unpinned it takes the mouse, which means it is also taking clicks away from the game": "",
  "Position": "",
  "set by dragging, not by typing. Unpin, move it, pin it": "",
  "Diagrams queue": "",
  "three corners are on screen at once — one leaving, one being talked about in the middle and drawn larger, one arriving. Whether they run across the screen or down it is about where you have put the panel; the position places the whole queue": "",
  "Queue direction": "",
  "which end corners arrive at and which end they leave from. A corner appears in the waiting place, moves to the middle when the coach gets to it, and on to the exit when the coach has finished — so this is the difference between reading the panel forwards and reading it backwards": "",
  "Speeds in": "",
  "no sim publishes which units its own dashboard is showing, so this has to be set to match. 120 reads as a plausible speed in either, so a mismatch does not announce itself": "",
  "Run the cars round": "",
  "a silhouette follows each line, to show how the car rotates through the corner. Both run in the same time rather than at their real speeds": "",
  "default (right-hand side, centred)": "",
